WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.837
Let's start off by talking about belongings in your daily life. Do you prefer having fewer or more belongings in your daily life? I think, personally, for me, I'd prefer having fewer belongings just because I feel like it would be more easier to deal with on a daily basis, especially when it comes to technology, trying to decide if I had a laptop and an iPad and a phone, I have all three of the same kind of thing. I just think it would be easier to function in life with less. What things can you not live without? My phone. I can't live without my phone. What else can't I live without? Water, always staying hydrated, and my cats. How do you organize your belongings? Sometimes I do it by color. If it was things related to books, I like to organize them by colors on the shelves, yeah. Now, let's talk about technology in modern life. How has technology changed your life? I think technology has changed my life in so many different ways, one of them being the ability to connect with people. Personally, I have a lot of long distance friends, and technology has allowed me to stay in touch with those people, and even though they're not with you personally, you do feel like you never miss that connection because of technology. I think work-related technology offers me a lot of opportunity to connect and network with many different kind of people. Would you like to have more technology in your life or less technology in your life? I personally would like to have less technology in my life just because I feel like technology sometimes defeats the purpose of humanity in a sense of if there's too much of it, like nowadays with the AI and all of that, it's kind of starting to replace human beings in a sense. I would like to depend less on technology, generally. Do you think technology has made your life better now than in the past when you had less technology? Yes, to a certain extent, just because I feel like back then, we didn't have Instagram, we didn't have those, the iconic social media platforms that all young teenagers use. And those are the main platforms people are using now to connect with people and to network, and especially if you're running your own business. For example, trying to share your music or share art, that's how you come out to a bigger platform. So definitely, I feel like it has a bigger advantage than what we had in the past. Now, let's talk about reading. Do you enjoy reading? I do enjoy reading, but I wish I did more of it. I feel like with technology and all the modern things coming up, I feel like I used to read more as a child, and I'm trying to get back into it. Some of the things I love reading about are self-help books. Those are my favorite. Now, let's talk about outdoor activities. What activities do you like to do outdoors? Outdoors, I love to go running sometimes. I like to do morning yogas. I've had yogas by the beach before. I really love joining group classes outdoors as well. I once did this thing where we started off with a morning yoga, and then it went into a HIIT workout, and then a yoga cool down. I love to go swimming. I love the ocean. I love to be by the beach. How often do you spend time outside? Not as often as I would like, just because given the location I'm in right now, It gets pretty hot and humid, and I wish our weather would accommodate being outdoors more often. But I try my best to be outside as much as possible. When you are outside, do you prefer being alone or with other people? I think I prefer being alone just because it gives you that space to be with yourself and to disconnect from everyone around you. For me, personally, sometimes I feel like when I'm around people, my social battery dies very quickly, and it could be a lot to take in. Both have their advantages and disadvantages. Of course, I'd love to hang out with my friends and be around my friends but, I recently did a solo trip to England, and I spent most of my time by myself in nature, walking around the city. I find that one learns a lot when they spend time with their self and the energies around them. In the future, would you like to do more activities outdoors? Yes, for sure. I always wanted to try out paragliding, skiing. Skiing has been one of like, an outdoor activity I'd always wanted to do. A time where I had to adapt to a new situation, it came when I graduated from high school and I had to finally move to college. And I was one of the people that decided ultimately to move out of my home place to go abroad for university just because I felt like university abroad had more resources and opportunity. It was pretty difficult to fit in. People have different culture over there, different outlook on life, but I needed to adapt because that was the only way I could grow in that environment. I think that some days were better than others. It was pretty difficult in the beginning just because I felt like... In the beginning, you feel very out of place. For example, when I started my degree, I didn't really had prior experience to the art world and everything related to the art world in my home country. So when I moved, I kind of was that one student in between everyone that was inexperienced or less experienced, and it got to me sometimes. Sometimes I cried in class. I had my breakdowns. But I think I learned a lot that you keep pushing through in those situations. It could be so easy to cave in to the bad or the negative. But if you just take it as a learning experience and keep moving, after a year, two years pass by, one starts to acclimate to their environment and one starts to adapt properly. We have been talking about a new situation you had to adapt to. We're going to continue to talk about change and adaptation and personal growth, things like that. Why do some people find it harder to adapt to change than others? To thank you for watching this video, I want to give you a free course that has helped thousands of students improve their IELTS speaking score. What it's going to do is take you through every single part of the test and give you strategies for part one, part two, and part three, and also allow you to practice at home for free and get feedback. To sign up for that for free, all you have to do is just click the link in the description. Thanks very much, and let's get back to the video. I think as human beings, we, we don't like change. I think humans like to stay the same. We grow up, our brain. Our brain, the way it's formulated, is that we get specific programs put into us when we're kids. And straying away from those kind of programs, whether it's the environment you grew up in or the relationships you've had throughout your life, I think it puts the human being in a state of fight or flight. That's why some people resist change or never want to change. Now, let's talk about cultural adaptation. Why do some people adapt more easily to living abroad than others? I think sometimes a person finds themselves abroad. Maybe that culture fits them more as a person, as a soul. I think that sometimes people find, they empathize with the people that they live with outside. And if one is reserved in their home country and decides to move out, they open themselves up to different experiences. And I think when one finds that freedom and that belonging somewhere else, they start to adapt a new lifestyle and way of living. What challenges do people face when adapting to a new culture? One of the many challenges I think people face when adapting to a new culture is sometimes there are some things about that culture that will not resonate with you as a person. For example, me personally, I decided to ultimately move out of America after I got my degree just because there were a couple of values in that culture that personally I didn't resonate with. And I found it better to find elsewhere, somewhere elsewhere, where I I could feel more at home and more like everything was in coherence with me. And last question in part three, how does facing challenges help people develop as individuals? I think facing challenges, most importantly, makes you listen to yourself more, makes you follow your gut more. And I'm a personal believer that nobody grows without pain and going through challenges. I think pain is a big life teacher. It's something that people have to go through in order to become a better version of themselves. Well done. I'm going to give you some feedback now. My feedback will be on part one and then part two, part three. So we're going to talk more strategic tactical things that you could do to ensure you get the highest score possible and that people watching at home can learn from that. And then we're also going to talk write your band score. So pronunciation, fluency, grammar, vocabulary, what would I give you? How would I help you get the highest score possible? So part one. To thank you for making it this far in the video, I want to give you 10% off our VIP course. The IELTS VIP course is the most successful IELTS course in the world. That is a fact because we have more Band 7, 8, and 9 success stories than any other IELTS course in the entire world. We do that by simplifying the whole IELTS process, supporting you with some of the best IELTS teachers in the world, and being with you every step of the way until you get the score that you need. All you have to do is just look down in the description, just click that, and you can sign up. If you have any questions about the VIP course, always feel free to get in touch with us. We answer 100% of the questions that we get. Hope that you would become a VIP. If not, enjoy the rest of this free video. Part one, you did something that many, many people do, which is you were quite nervous in the beginning, and that affected your fluency a little bit. Myself, as a native English speaker, if I was doing this test, the first few answers probably wouldn't be the most fluent ones because you are a little nervous in the beginning. That is why in part one, they don't start off with the really difficult questions. They just throw a little getting to know you questions at you. But you coped pretty well after that. So as you went on, your answers became more fluent and you became less nervous. And a good examiner will recognize that. A lot of people think that if you have one bad answer, that you immediately get a low score. That's not how the test works. The examiner will base your score on the totality of your answers. For example, you gave some very short answers at the beginning, or you just were like, oh, listed lots of different things. The examiner knows that's just nerves based on your subsequent answers. So pick the things that are easy to talk about, and it's okay to have some nerves. Just understand that that's part of the test. Part two, a similar thing at the beginning of part two, you could tell that you were thinking a lot. You were trying to come up with the best ideas, I think. Is that true or not? Yes. So remember that it's not an ideas test. It is a speaking test. A lot of people think that they'll get a higher score or sound more impressive if they choose the best ideas. The best ideas are the ones that are easiest for you to talk about, the simplest ideas. At the beginning, your fluency, it wasn't bad or anything like that, but it's just that you were, you could tell that the cogs in your brain were working overtime trying to think of the correct thing, and our brains are obviously connected to our speech. And if our brains are overworked, just like a computer with too many tabs open, then the speech coming out will be affected sometimes. It wasn't that affected, but again, the examiner won't listen to the first few sentences and go, terrible fluency. Not that it was terrible, but a lot of people watching think that that's the case. They've been taught in their school that if they don't sound fluent the entire time, that they're going to get a lower score. That is not the case. But overall, your part two was great. Some great vocabulary, some great grammar. But on test day, you might get a question that you don't feel comfortable talking about or is just unusual for you. That could affect your fluency because unusual topic plus overthinking could lead to you just having fluency issues. So again, my advice is pretty repetitive, but talk about the things that are easy to talk about. Know that it's not a performance, it's a conversation. All right? So sometimes it felt like you were performing rather than talking to me. Everybody is different. Some people don't like making eye contact, but when you were thinking, you were looking away and looking up. A lot of students do that. They always look up. Maybe that's part of your brain that deals with ideas, and you can tell they're really thinking deeply. When you did that, you were thinking a lot and your fluency suffered at times. You did this thing as well. You did it at the start of part two and you did it in part three, where you think and speak at the same time and you usually repeat the question. So what you do is you're like, so describe a time when you had to adapt to a new situation. So you're talking and thinking all at the same time and repeating the question. A better way might be to just give yourself a little bit more time. You don't have to answer immediately. Think about it for a second or two and just say, that's an interesting question. I haven't thought about that one before, or that's a really good question. Let me think about that for a second and then give your answer. Because some examiners might think that you're just repeating the question back at them. So that's not really showing how great your English is, and your fluency is not great at the same time. The first 10, 20% of your answers, the fluency seems to be lower than the final 80, 90% because you're thinking what to say. Yes. Does that make sense? Yes. Is that helpful? You probably don't even know you're doing it. It's probably just a habit. And in normal life, you probably don't need to do that, but we want to make sure that you get one of the top marks. Another way that you could improve, especially your part three, would be to include more examples. So you talk generally about situations that happen, like when you were crying in college or the values that you disagreed with. Maybe you didn't want to say that because you don't want to be controversial or whatever. Maybe you want to not offend anyone. But in the real test, the cameras are not rolling. Feel free to give those real examples because they're often easier to talk about. Another way is just to imagine you're talking to someone who doesn't know anything about what you're talking about and you need to help them understand. So using examples is just a way that human beings help, it's just a communications tool. Explanations are communications tools. Again, nothing bad, but we want to make sure that you get one of the top marks, and these are just me being overly cautious and helping the people learn at home as well. So what scores would you get? The best part of your performance is your pronunciation. Even though you didn't grow up in an English-speaking country, you have picked up the accent. You don't sound like an American per se, but you don't sound like you're from Lebanon or Iraq either. Now, you don't get extra marks for sounding American, but you do get extra marks for being as clear as possible. And the thing about the American accent, not a lot of people know this, but the reason why the American accent is generally clear is because so many people from so many different parts of the world move to America. So everybody developed this accent that is super clear. So it's actually a good accent to have for communication. Standard American accent. Standard American accent. Most people in the world will be able to understand you. Whereas me, I'm Irish, not everyone understands the Irish accent. So I'm not saying that anybody watching needs to move to America or learn the American accent, but having that accent for you, it doesn't sound forced. It doesn't sound like you're putting it on. It sounds like that's your natural accent, and it really does help communication. The other thing that spending time in America has helped you do is to sound more like a native English speaker with things like intonation, connected speech, word stress. This is where we show meaning through, for example, our voice is going up or our voice is going down, like for example, when we're listing things, I like sushi and I like Italian food and I like steak, and I also like Indian food. So I went up and I went up and I went up, and then I went down at the end. I'm listing, I'm listing, I'm listing, now I'm done. So I'm conveying meaning through tone, not through actual words. And you do that very, very well. So Band 9 for pronunciation. Very, very good. You also have amazing vocabulary. Things that many English speakers would not be able to use. One I noted down was draining a social battery. I really like that. That was quite poetic. The reason why that is so good is that you used fewer words to explain something quite complicated. Draining of a social battery, what does that actually mean? It means that you are maybe an introverted person, that when you're around other people, you have less energy. You like to be alone and that boosts your energy a little bit. That took me, what, three or four sentences to explain that, or I can just say something like, it drains my social battery. So it is very precise and very economical and elegant, and you had lots of examples of that like this. For vocabulary, you would definitely get a Band 9. Grammar, you do make some little, tiny slips like this. That is totally normal, even for native English speakers. A lot of people think that if you make one tiny little grammatical error, you're going to get a Band 5 or a Band 6 or something like that. That is not true because even as an educated native English speaker who teaches English for a living, I make a little grammatical errors. Every English speaker does. Even English teachers make little grammatical errors. And in the marking criteria, it actually says for band 9, makes slips very infrequently. That's exactly you. You do make tiny little errors, but very infrequently. So you would get a Band 9 for grammar. Okay. Now, your weakest area is your fluency. This is related to the things that we talked about at the beginning, which is being nervous at the start. So there were a lot of mms, ahhs, like, kinda we would call them audible pauses or fillers. Again, even high-level native English speakers use those little words when we're nervous. I'm doing it right now. So don't worry too much about it. But for the purposes of an IELTS test, the less nervous that you are, the better your fluency is going to be. Also, you have that issue of when you're thinking, it slows down the pace that you're speaking at, and you also have more of those audible pauses. Nerves can be solved quite easily through preparation. So doing stuff like this with me and me giving you feedback, you're going to be a lot less nervous going into the real test because you're like, I've been there, done that, I've practiced so much. So it's going to be a walk in the park for you. And the other thing is, try less to perform and more conversation. I know it's difficult. Not every examiner is someone that you would consider a friend, but for 15 minutes, pretend you're talking to a friend in a coffee shop. Literally picture that friend or that family member, and they're asking you these questions. And I think that will really, really help your fluency. Coherence is fine. You're answering every question. You're developing them enough. I think examples could also help with that. Just pick really, really simple things. There's nothing in the marking criteria that talks about levels of ideas. You don't get extra marks for good ideas. You get extra marks for pronunciation, fluency, grammar, vocabulary. So everything that you do, all the advice that I'm giving you is designed to help those things. And for someone like you at a very, very high level, it is more important that we simplify your ideas because you're not thinking about English so much. You're thinking about how do I give them the best answer, the best idea, and that can actually hinder your fluency. So that would be... But overall, I think you would get a Band 9. But on a bad day, let's say you didn't sleep the night before and you didn't get a good breakfast and you're late, and then you get a question that you're not happy with and you try to overperform, that could drop you down. But I don't think it's going to happen, but well done. Thank you.


